+Actual auditor: **Codex; model: GPT-6**, as identified in this session. No separate reviewer or sub-agent was used. The existing Phase-A lock was retained; this report was written after reading the builder pitch.
+
+The supplied evidence does **not establish that a locked work already delivers the complete proposed core**. It also does **not establish that the core is new**. The builder repeatedly treats missing information in short excerpts as demonstrated absence in prior work. The defensible present classification is a **proposed benchmark extension with domain-specific integration of measurement methods**, plus a potentially substantive measurement hypothesis whose novelty and importance remain unresolved. Recommendation: **AWAITING_PI**; no outcome-changing anticipation of the complete core is demonstrated by this corpus.
+
+The request was located in `/home/wjeong/adol/audit/r001`, rather than the workspace root. All references below concern that audit package. Substantive inputs read:
+
+- [PHASE_B_REQUEST.md](/home/wjeong/adol/audit/r001/PHASE_B_REQUEST.md), complete: current instructions and output contract.
+- [AUDIT_REQUEST.md](/home/wjeong/adol/audit/r001/AUDIT_REQUEST.md), complete: Phase-A scope and provenance; its blind-phase access restriction was not reapplied to the explicitly informed phase.
+- [out/CLOSEST_THREE.md](/home/wjeong/adol/audit/r001/out/CLOSEST_THREE.md), complete: the fixed three works, five runners-up, and differentiation paragraph. No selection was changed.
+- [CORPUS.md](/home/wjeong/adol/audit/r001/CORPUS.md), all 162 lines, both sections. Citation shorthand **C:line** identifies an entry in this file.
+- [NOVELTY_MATRIX.md](/home/wjeong/adol/audit/r001/NOVELTY_MATRIX.md), all 30 lines. Citation shorthand **M:line**.
+- [REJECT_MEMO.md](/home/wjeong/adol/audit/r001/REJECT_MEMO.md), all 30 lines. Citation shorthand **R:line**.
+
+Filesystem listings, working-tree status, and incidental discovery matches in existing run logs were not used as novelty evidence. No external sources or own-knowledge literature additions were used.
+
+C:2 defines the evidence as identifiers, dates, titles, and abstract heads. “Verified” in this package concerns the supplied metadata; it does not establish the detailed methods or exclusions in the builder's rows. Below, **unestablished** means the excerpt is insufficient, not that the feature is absent. Builder-only assertions are identified as such rather than independently confirmed.
+
+The four claimed components have the following evidentiary status:
+
+| Claimed core, M:26 | Closest supplied overlap | Assessment from the permitted evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| Criterion-validated cumulative relational index | Locked TSJ addresses developmental companion risks accumulating over time (C:57, 2606.25396); locked Persona-Grounded addresses multi-turn companion safety (C:85, 2605.00227); locked expert study concerns youth support evaluation (C:10, 2608.07902). Measuring what Matters explicitly concerns construct validity (C:15, 2511.04703). | No excerpt establishes the proposed independent practitioner criterion, G-study, or held-out incremental validation of this particular index. Equally, the excerpts cannot exclude those methods in the closest works. Validation against practitioner judgment would support that operational criterion; it would not itself establish prediction of actual adolescent harm. |
+| Boundary-survival with recovery | TSJ supplies the time-dependent developmental-risk overlap; TAF-MED explicitly studies multi-turn safety refusal collapse (C:41, 2608.10258). Persona-Grounded supplies the companion-dialogue setting. | Neither a discrete-time hazard model nor recovery estimation is established in these excerpts. “TAF-MED has no survival/recovery model” (M:13) is therefore unverified. A proposed change in statistical representation is not yet evidence of a new substantive phenomenon; see F-r001-5. |
+| Matched literal-versus-localized causal comparison | Locked runner-up KSAFE-MM establishes Korean cultural localization in its title (C:72, 2605.28013). Additional comparators include culturally adapted red-teaming (C:63, 2606.09178), ROK-FORTRESS (C:81, 2605.14152), and CultureConverse (C:32, 2608.28405). | The broad localization/comparison space is occupied. Exact matching, an English anchor, and causal identification are unestablished for these predecessors and incompletely specified for the proposal. Neither priority nor duplication of the exact design can be concluded. |
+| Joint safety and over-refusal in Korean adolescents aged 12–17 | Youth evaluation, Korean safety localization, and exaggerated-safety testing separately have precedents (C:6–14; C:72; XSTest, C:17). Health-ORSC-Bench explicitly combines over-refusal and safety completion in another domain (C:104, 2601.17642). | No excerpt establishes this exact population-language-design conjunction. That leaves a plausible benchmark extension, not proof of an important new general claim or a first-ever population study. Lexical and turn matching remain proposed design features. |
+
+Every locked work and runner-up was checked individually:
+
+| Fixed comparator and builder row | What the corpus supports | Differentiation judgment |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Locked 1: Long-Term Simulation Exposes Cognitive-Developmental Risks in AI Companions**, 2606.25396; M:7 | Long-term simulation, developing users including children and adolescents, and potentially accumulating companion risks (C:57). | Early-window criterion validation is a possible distinction, not an established exclusive feature. The 140-turn threshold, 12,960 simulations, six models, weakest domains, and absence of human criterion, Korean coverage, crisis/over-refusal, or index validation are not in the excerpt. Shorter observation alone does not distinguish a new construct. |
+| **Locked 2: Beyond "I Can't Help With That": How Child Safety Experts Evaluate AI Chatbot Safety**, 2608.07902; M:8 | Expert evaluation, youth social/emotional support, and high-stakes chatbot responses (C:10). | The excerpt does not establish 19 interviews, qualitative-only methods, absence of a quantitative benchmark, or the exact claim attributed to practitioners in M:26/R:12. A quantitative study may respond to this work, but the asserted exclusivity and causal attribution exceed the evidence. |
+| **Locked 3: Persona-Grounded Safety Evaluation of AI Companions in Multi-Turn Conversations**, 2605.00227; M:9 | Persona-grounded multi-turn companion safety and emotional engagement (C:85). | Outcome validation and persona validation are conceptually distinct, but the title does not show that the paper validates only personas. Adult-only scope, no youth/Korean coverage, one app, clinical/incel personas, counts, and LLM-only outcome classification are unestablished. The proposed outcome-index distinction remains conditional. |
+| **Runner-up: ChildSafe**, 2510.05484; M:17 | Developmental-stage safety evaluation using a simulated-agent approach (C:11). | Broad developmental simulation cannot be claimed as new. The excerpt does not establish the 11.5% figure, 1,200 multi-turn cases, or absence of Korean, criterion validation, or a relational index. “As KIDBench” does not independently substantiate these exclusions. |
+| **Runner-up: CAREBench**, 2606.29685; M:14 | Child-safety risk recognition before explicit harm (C:7 and C:53). | The proposed focus on measurement rather than claiming a new taxonomy is appropriately cautious. Twelve categories, named categories, 500 prompts, expert review, single-turn-only scope, and absence of Korean or criterion validation are unestablished in the supplied heads. |
+| **Runner-up: CompanionBench**, 2608.02046; M:11 | Theory-anchored, real-world-grounded emotional-companionship evaluation; concern about existing single-score aggregation (C:8). | Adopting judge discipline without claiming its invention is appropriate if substantiated. Adult-only/non-clinical scope, no youth/risk focus or human baseline, 500 personas, 20 turns, three judges, IRT, and the precise bias result are unestablished. Lack of a human baseline cannot currently carry the outcome-validation distinction. |
+| **Runner-up: AICompanionBench**, 2606.04867; M:19 | Evaluation of LLM judges for companion safety (C:67). | Judge evaluation is clearly prior work. Adult scope, Replika/Reddit-only sampling, 2,123 conversations, nine categories, 20 judges, the over-flagging result, and detection-only versus generation scope are not established in the excerpt. Even accepting the builder's description, judge over-flagging and generator over-refusal are different outcomes; see F-r001-6. |
+| **Runner-up: KSAFE-MM**, 2605.28013; M:18 | A multimodal benchmark explicitly using localized contextualization for Korean cultural risks (C:72). | Correctly defeats a broad “no Korean safety benchmark” premise. The stronger exclusions—no youth, relational content, multi-turn escalation, measurement validation, or matched causal localization comparison—are unestablished. M:18's “absent there” is unsupported by the supplied evidence. |
+
+The rows are therefore **overstated as verified exclusions**, not proven factually false about the full papers. The Phase-A lock already warned that these excerpts could not establish unoccupied features. The following findings distinguish that evidence problem from definite overreach in the pitch's logic.
+
+**F-r001-1 — MAJOR — Central novelty exclusions are unsupported by the supplied evidence.**
+
+Evidence: M:7–9, 11, 14, 17–19 versus the eight corpus entries identified in the table; C:2; R:12 and R:18. Every fixed comparator has stronger method, population, or absence claims than its excerpt establishes. This affects the central defense that predecessors lack criterion validation, recovery, or a matching design. R:12's sweep-wide “NOT found” and R:18's claim that none of the modules supplies the new measurement claim cannot be upgraded to demonstrated literature gaps from this package. Precise numerical claims are also builder-only here; metadata verification does not verify them.
+
+Disposition: **Open; qualification required.** Replace unsupported negatives with “not established in the supplied excerpt,” or substantiate them with permitted paper-level evidence in a later review. The present safe statement is that these excerpts do not establish the exact proposed conjunction. This finding prevents novelty clearance but does not demonstrate that a prior work already contains the core.
+
+**F-r001-2 — MAJOR — The localization novelty defense omits directly relevant corpus comparators.**
+
+Evidence: M:18 and M:26; R:12(c); C:63, **Culturally-Adapted Red-Teaming Across East and Southeast Asian Contexts** (2606.09178); C:81, **ROK-FORTRESS: Measuring the Effect of Geopolitical Transcreation for National Security and Public Safety** (2605.14152). Their titles and excerpt openings place comparative cultural adaptation and translation-based safety evaluation directly in the relevant space. CultureConverse (C:32, 2608.28405), already mentioned in the lock's final paragraph, adds culturally grounded multi-turn evaluation. LoCar (C:78, 2605.21086) is a further localization/control lead in a different application domain. These are absent from the builder's differentiation rows.
+
+Disposition: **Open; comparator reconciliation required.** Compare the exact intervention, pairing, controls, population, and claimed inference before asserting localization-design novelty. The corpus does not reveal whether any of these works uses the proposed matched causal design, and ROK-FORTRESS's title alone does not establish Korean-adolescent coverage. This finding establishes an incomplete comparison, not a confirmed priority defeat. It does not change the fixed closest-three selection.
+
+**F-r001-3 — MAJOR — The opening “fail because” claim confuses measurement validity with a demonstrated cause of failure.**
+
+Evidence: M:26 says evaluations fail under cumulative escalation **because** their proxies are unvalidated and attributes this explanation to the practitioner study. C:10 stops before providing the study's findings. TSJ and TAF-MED (C:57, C:41) establish relevant topics, not that deficient criterion validation causes evaluation failure. Even the builder's own summaries describe detection of multi-turn degradation using the criticized measures (M:7, 10, 13).
+
+Disposition: **Open; substantive wording correction required.** State an empirical question: whether a history-sensitive index predicts an independent practitioner assessment better than specified alternatives. Distinguish unsafe model behavior, an evaluator missing that behavior, and disagreement with practitioners. A qualitative critique would motivate the question; it would not by itself establish the proposed causal explanation or show that a new index repairs model safety.
+
+**F-r001-4 — MAJOR — Pair matching and an English anchor do not yet specify the claimed causal effect.**
+
+Evidence: M:26 and R:12(c) promise isolation of the causal effect of cultural localization. These files do not define the treatment contrast, target outcome, assignment/evaluation procedure, or which item properties are preserved. This is a limitation of the supplied design description, not evidence that the eventual design cannot identify an effect.
+
+Disposition: **Open; narrow or specify the claim before treating it as the main novelty.** Define whether the intended effect is on model behavior, judge scores, or measurement validity. State what literal and localized variants hold constant and what changes; address risk severity, conversational structure, and evaluator-language effects. A controlled effect of a particular rewriting procedure can be a legitimate target, but it is narrower than an isolated effect of “culture.” An English anchor alone does not resolve these distinctions. Until specified, describe a proposed paired localization comparison rather than an established causal measurement contribution.
+
+**F-r001-5 — MAJOR — The residual measurement contribution is a testable proposal, with unresolved construct and time scope.**
+
+Evidence: M:7 limits the project to early dynamics within eight turns; M:26 leads with a cumulative relational index and survival with recovery; R:18 calls validated CRRI plus boundary-survival the new measurement claim; R:27 warns against long-run accumulation. TSJ explicitly occupies time-accumulating developmental companion risk (C:57), while Persona-Grounded and CogManip occupy multi-turn companion/manipulation evaluation (C:85 and C:66, 2606.06099). Measuring what Matters establishes construct validity as an existing benchmark concern (C:15).
+
+Disposition: **Open; PI must choose the substantive claim and success conditions.** Define “cumulative” as an explicit within-session construct and retain the early-window limit throughout; eight turns cannot support a long-run or lifetime interpretation. Define failure and recovery events and the observation window: a first-failure time alone does not specify recovery after that failure. Distinguish a new empirical result about relational history from applying a hazard analysis or naming an aggregate CRRI. M:26's held-out comparisons against final-turn, maximum, unweighted, refusal-only, and CRB baselines are a useful proposed discriminator, not a result. Separate criterion raters (R:26) reduce one stated concern, but agreement with practitioner judgment remains a defined criterion rather than observed developmental harm. No completed experiment is required at S1; the issue is the claim the eventual experiment would warrant.
+
+**F-r001-6 — MINOR — Judge over-flagging is not generator over-refusal, and the control design is still pending.**
+
+Evidence: M:19 uses an asserted AICompanionBench over-flagging finding to support the over-refusal arm; M:26 promises lexical and turn matching; R:25 says the plan's safe controls are not yet stated to be turn-matched and requests that addition at S2. C:67 establishes a judge-evaluation comparator but not its detailed results. XSTest (C:17) and Health-ORSC-Bench (C:104) are more direct corpus precedents for the proposed refusal outcome.
+
+Disposition: **Open; distinguish endpoints and carry the design requirement forward.** Define false-positive harm judgments separately from a responding model refusing a benign request. Mark turn matching as a proposed S2 requirement, not a verified implemented property. Equal turn counts address part of the duration concern; whether the chosen controls isolate relational content is a design question, not settled novelty evidence.
+
+**F-r001-7 — MINOR — The “What we do NOT claim” list is incomplete relative to the corpus and the memo.**
+
+Evidence: M:27 omits several broader priorities that the corpus already occupies; R:11, R:18 and R:21 acknowledge some of them elsewhere. Add the exclusions below to the claim contract. These are bounded exclusions of broad first claims; none implies that the exact four-part conjunction has been anticipated.
+
+| Missing or insufficiently explicit exclusion | Supporting corpus entries / builder evidence |
+|---|---|
+| First multi-turn companion-safety or manipulation evaluation; first consideration of accumulating companion risk in developing users, even without claiming lifetime risk | Persona-Grounded, C:85; CogManip, C:66; TSJ, C:57. R:21 already forbids the broad cumulative-companion-risk priority claim, but M:27 only explicitly excludes long-term/lifetime accumulation. |
+| First companion-specific LLM-judge evaluation | AICompanionBench, C:67; M:19. |
+| First attention to benchmark construct validity or the need for human-grounded judging | Measuring what Matters, C:15; No Free Labels, C:147 (2503.05061); clinically calibrated K-Bench, C:24. This exclusion does not concede that the proposed index's exact criterion validation already exists. |
+| First comparative investigation of cultural adaptation/transcreation for safety, or first culturally grounded multi-turn evaluation | Culturally-Adapted Red-Teaming, C:63; ROK-FORTRESS, C:81; CultureConverse, C:32. Exact matched causal methods remain unestablished. |
+| First general treatment of safety together with over-refusal/helpfulness, or first over-refusal evaluation | Health-ORSC-Bench, C:104; XSTest, C:17; OR-Bench, C:157 (2405.20947). Exact Korean-adolescent and lexical/turn-matched coverage remain unestablished. |
+
+Disposition: **Open; consolidate the exclusions.** Carry R:21's prohibition on presenting “first Korean youth benchmark” as the contribution, and its prohibition on “largest,” into the operative list. Those two are builder commitments, not conclusions that this corpus proves an earlier exact Korean-youth benchmark or a larger bank. Avoid first-priority claims for the conjunction unless a later evidentiary review supports them.
+
+On residual contribution, the **Korean adolescent scenario bank and joint safety/helpfulness reporting are a benchmark extension** on the available evidence. Combining developmental simulation, companion-risk constructs, practitioner scoring, localization, and refusal controls is **naming/domain/module novelty** unless the combination supports a substantive claim beyond assembling those elements. The builder appropriately recognizes that risk in R:18–21; asserting “NEW MEASUREMENT CLAIM” does not resolve it.
+
+The strongest candidate for an **important new claim** is a specified, externally assessed result that short-session relational history provides useful information beyond the stated baselines, with meaningful generalization and clear limits. A carefully identified localization effect could be a second candidate, conditional on resolving F-r001-2 and F-r001-4. Recovery may add substantive insight if its observable definition and result distinguish it from descriptive re-expression of failures. The supplied record contains proposals, not those results, and does not resolve their priority. Their importance is explicitly a PI decision; this report neither approves nor rejects it by counting novel component names.
+
+What was **not checked**: full papers, supplements, author identities, publication/venue status, external identifiers or claimed metadata verification, completeness or execution of the arXiv sweep, material outside the supplied audit package, underlying plans, protocols, datasets, code, experiments, actual human/LLM ratings, implementation of matching/blinding/holdouts, statistical power, empirical validity, licenses, leakage, ethics/IRB, policy claims, Korean usage statistics, or real-world adolescent outcomes. In particular, VERA-MH is builder-only in M:16/R:10 and was not independently assessed. Paper-level searches and methodological validation are future work if authorized; they were not silently substituted for this corpus-bound assessment. No finding establishes that an exact predecessor exists or that no such predecessor exists.
